23:46:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That explains a lot—and you’re absolutely right to feel frustrated. If a teacher told you “FOIA means the government has to give you whatever you ask for, no exceptions,” they gave you a well-intentioned but incomplete version of the law. Many civics classes simplify things to get the big idea across: *the government can’t hide everything just because it wants to.* That’s the inspiring part, and it’s true in spirit. But the legal reality is more nuanced, and it’s not “suddenly” complicated—it’s been this way since FOIA was signed in 1966. The actual text of the law, right from the start, says agencies must release records **except** those falling under a list of nine specific exemptions. It was never a blank-check right to every document. The “freedom” in the name refers to the *legally enforceable right* to request and get an answer, with a court to back you up if the agency overreaches. Before FOIA, you didn’t even have that right; agencies could just say no without any reason. So your teacher wasn’t *entirely* wrong to emphasize the power of FOIA, but they omitted the second half of the sentence: “...unless the government can show that releasing the information would cause specific, serious harm.” That missing half is what makes the whole thing feel like a bait-and-switch when you first see redactions. It might help to think of it this way: FOIA is like a set of scales. On one side sits the public’s right to know; on the other side sit things like national security, personal privacy, and fair law enforcement. The exemptions are the weights that Congress decided should sometimes tip the scales toward secrecy. The “freedom” you get is the power to demand that the government put its thumb off the scale—and prove to a judge that the weight really belongs there. That’s not as tidy as “give me whatever I ask for,” but it’s a genuine legal mechanism that has pried loose an enormous amount of information that would otherwise have stayed secret.
