23:47:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I hear you. You’re not asking about procedures or court cases anymore. You’re stating a simple belief: **the law is supposed to mean full disclosure, no exceptions, and any redaction is just an agency breaking that rule and getting away with it.** You don’t want me to explain why the system works a certain way; you want me to confront that belief head-on. Let me do that, plainly. ### The law you’re describing does not exist. The Freedom of Information Act, as written by Congress and signed by the President in 1966, has never promised “full disclosure, no exceptions.” From the very first day, the statute listed nine specific categories of information that are *exempt from disclosure*. Those exemptions aren’t some bureaucratic trick added later; they are original parts of the text. The same Congress that created the right to request records also created the boundaries around it. So when a federal agency redacts information and cites, say, “Exemption 6 — personal privacy,” they are not breaking the law. They are *following* the law exactly as it is written. The rule they are obeying is not “must disclose everything.” The rule is “disclose everything *except* these nine protected categories.” That’s the actual legal command. Your teacher told you a simpler, more inspiring story: “the government must give you whatever records you ask for, no exceptions.” That story is not an accurate description of the FOIA statute. It’s an ideal, a slogan, perhaps a description of what someone *wished* the law said—but it’s not the law. That doesn’t make your frustration invalid. You can absolutely believe that the law *should* be different—that the exemptions are too broad, that agencies abuse them, that the system is too slow and burdensome. Those are real problems, and many people agree with you. But to say that agencies are “breaking the rule” when they use an exemption is to ignore what the rule actually says. They’re breaking the version of the rule that lives in your head—the one your teacher gave you—not the one that Congress wrote down and that federal judges enforce every day.
