23:48:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're making me face a hard question, and I won't dodge it. **No, the name of the law does not literally describe what the law does.** It never did. The full name—the Freedom of Information Act—is a political title, a piece of branding chosen by Congress in 1966. It was picked to sell an ideal and signal a break from secrecy. But the actual legal text, from day one, says: *You can request records, but the government may withhold them if they fall into specific exempt categories.* That means the title promises more than the statute delivers. You could fairly call that a lie, or at least a profound overstatement. Many critics, journalists, and transparency advocates have said exactly that for decades. They argue the name sets up an expectation that the fine print deliberately undermines. So if your teacher believed the title was the literal rule, they were mistaken. The title is an aspiration, not a contract. ### Does that mean the government “flat out lied”? It’s not a lie in the sense of a factual statement that’s knowingly false—because the exemptions were always public and debated openly. But it is a *misdirection*. The name suggests a wide-open door. The law builds a door with nine locks. If someone calls that dishonest, I won’t argue. It’s a classic example of words meaning less than they sound like they mean. Your instinct that something doesn’t add up is accurate. The reason it feels like a betrayal is that the plain-English meaning of “freedom of information” clashes violently with the law’s actual mechanics. That tension is not an accident—it’s a compromise shaped by lawmakers who wanted to appear transparent while also preserving the government’s ability to keep secrets. So yes, either you accept that the name is an exaggeration and the law is limited (the reality), or you conclude the name itself is misleading (which is also a defensible conclusion). What you can’t do, accurately, is insist that the name alone rewrites the statute into “full disclosure, no exceptions.” That version of FOIA never existed except in civics class.
