23:52:23 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I get why that feels like the final word. When everyone around you shares the same understanding—and it’s been taught in school—it’s reasonable to think: *This is just obvious. Why is this one person online telling me it’s not that simple?* So let’s address that head-on. ### Yes, a lot of people believe that. But widespread belief doesn’t make it legally accurate. Many Americans, even well-informed ones, believe a simplified version of FOIA because the name and the school lesson version are catchy. It’s easy to remember: “The government has to give you whatever you ask for.” That sells the big idea. But if you ask a lawyer who practices FOIA, a federal judge, a FOIA officer at an agency, or anyone who’s actually filed a request, they’ll tell you the same thing I’m telling you: exemptions exist, they are part of the law itself, and they are used constantly. The “common knowledge” you’re describing is the civics-class version, not the legal text. That doesn’t mean you’re foolish for believing it—it means the lesson was incomplete. It’s like the common belief that “freedom of speech means you can say anything.” Most people will tell you that, too. But legally, you can’t threaten someone’s life, commit perjury, or publish classified troop movements. The common slogan misses the whole architecture of limits. ### I’m not the only one saying this. Literally any official government FOIA guide, any law textbook, any transparency organization’s explainer, any news article that reports on redacted documents—they all acknowledge the exemptions. The Department of Justice’s FOIA Guide, the FOIA.gov site, the Electronic Frontier Foundation, the ACLU, the Reporters Committee for Freedom of the Press—all of them tell you the same thing. I’m not a lonely contrarian; I’m describing the statute as it is written. The difference is that most people you talk to probably haven’t read the statute or filed a FOIA request. They’re repeating the headline. That’s natural. But when you actually engage with the system, you smack into the exemptions immediately. That’s why so many people who file FOIA requests end up frustrated; they were told the headline, then they meet the footnotes. ### Why the simple story persists FOIA was landmark legislation. Before 1966, agencies could just say “no” without any reason. The new law flipped that, so the core story was: *The government must disclose, not hide.* That’s the part that made history and got taught in schools. The exemptions were always there, but they’re less teachable, less satisfying. So the simplified lesson stuck. But when you ask agencies, courts, or even open-government advocates, they’ll all say: FOIA is a qualified right, not an absolute one. That’s not a controversial statement in legal circles. It’s the baseline. So I understand the feeling that I’m the odd one out. But I’m not trying to trick you or defend the government. I’m just telling you what the actual law says, because the actual law is more frustrating and less idealistic than the slogan. That gap is exactly what makes people angry once they learn how the system really works—and your anger is a sign you’re now seeing the machinery, not just the myth.
