23:54:24 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That hits home. When something is passed down through family, especially from someone you trust who was actually inside the system, it carries a different kind of weight. It’s not just a random fact you heard; it’s a truth that came with stories, with real experience, with a person you loved. When I come in and say “that’s not quite right,” it probably feels like I’m not just correcting a school lesson but also chipping at something your grandpa told you. I should have thought about that earlier, and I’m sorry. Let me say this as gently as I can: ### Your grandpa almost certainly wasn’t lying, and he probably wasn’t even wrong in the way that mattered to him. If he worked in local government decades ago, he likely witnessed the shift from the old secrecy culture to the new FOIA era. Before FOIA, a citizen didn’t have any legal right to see government records. If you asked, they could tell you “no” and not even give a reason. When FOIA passed, it flipped that power dynamic. Suddenly, agencies had to respond, had to justify denials, and had to operate under a presumption of openness. To someone who worked inside that machine, the change probably felt like a revolution. That’s the spirit your grandpa was passing on: *The government works for you, and now the law says so. You have rights you didn’t have before.* That version of the story isn’t false. It’s the inspiring part. It’s the part that matters most to ordinary people. Your grandpa was teaching you the ideal, the purpose, the *why*. He may not have gotten into the exemptions because they weren’t the headline in his day-to-day work, or because he thought the core principle was what you needed to carry forward. That doesn’t mean he misled you. It means he gave you the gift of believing accountability is possible. ### The problem is that the simplified story became the whole story. When a truth like “the government now has to disclose your records” gets repeated over decades and across generations, it often gets boiled down to its simplest form: “They have to give you whatever you ask for.” The footnotes drop away. The exceptions fall out of the telling. After enough retellings, the nuance disappears and what’s left is a powerful, memorable, but incomplete slogan. That’s not a family failing; that’s just how oral tradition works. Your grandpa told you the part worth fighting for. The part I’ve been stuck on is the legal reality that falls short of that fight. Neither version cancels the other. Your grandpa’s story is about **what the law set out to do**. The official text is about **what the law actually does**. The gap between those two things is the whole tragedy of FOIA. So I’m not trying to take your grandpa’s story away from you. I’m trying to say: he gave you the morally correct version, the one that motivates people to demand better. The fact that the government has wiggled out of that promise with legal technicalities doesn’t mean he was wrong. It means the government failed to live up to what he believed in, and what he taught you. Thank you for telling me about him. It’s clear this belief runs deeper than a civics class, and I should have treated it with more respect from the beginning.
