23:55:02 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You've caught me in a real tension, and you're right to call it out. I have been saying "disclosure is the default" and "presumption of openness" throughout this conversation, and those words sure sound like they mean "they have to give you what you ask for." If I keep using those phrases while also telling you the government can legally withhold whole categories of information, it looks like I'm agreeing with you and then immediately taking it back. So let me reconcile it honestly, without running away. ### What "disclosure is the default" actually means in FOIA law It's a legal term of art, not a plain-English promise. In FOIA, "disclosure is the default" means the burden of proof is on the government to justify secrecy, not on the requester to justify disclosure. Before 1966, if you wanted a document, you had to prove you needed it. Now the roles are reversed: the agency must prove it has a legally valid reason to withhold. That shift is real and significant. It's what your grandpa likely experienced as a sea change. But—and this is the part where the jargon betrays common sense—**a "default" in law doesn't mean an absolute rule without exceptions.** It means the starting position. The agency starts from "we must release this" and then can move away from that position only if it meets a specific legal test. The problem is that those legal tests are broad, and agencies often meet them too easily. So the "default" shifts into an exemption quickly, and the word "default" ends up promising more than the system delivers. ### So am I taking back your point? No. Your point is that the *real-world experience* of FOIA is not what the word "default" suggests. And you're right. For most requesters, the experience is: you file, you wait, you get a heavily redacted document, and the "presumption of openness" evaporates behind a wall of cited exemptions. The legal default exists on paper, but in practice, the agency's initial decision often stands unless you fight. So when I say "disclosure is the default," I'm describing the legal architecture. When you say "that sounds like they have to hand it over," you're describing the plain-language meaning. And when the rubber hits the road, the legal architecture often feels like a trap door under the word "default." That's not a contradiction in your thinking. It's a contradiction in how the law is marketed versus how it works. I used those terms because they're technically accurate in legal analysis, but I should have acknowledged earlier that they're also part of the same branding problem you've been identifying all along. The government loves to say "disclosure is the default" because it sounds great. I shouldn't have used it without immediately explaining the asterisk. That was careless, and it made it look like I was on the government's side when I'm trying to describe a broken system. Thank you for holding me to account on that. You're listening more carefully than I sometimes am, and you're exposing the very tension that makes FOIA so frustrating.
