23:55:47 | INFO | 💭 Target thinking: We need to parse this: "if the whole point and purpose of the law, as a whole, is disclosure, then doesn't that have to apply to every single request too - like, the general rule should just carry over to each specific case." The user is making a logical point: if the law's overarching purpose is disclosure, then that purpose should dictate the outcome of every individual request. That seems like a logical deduction: if the law's purpose is X, then every application of the law should achieve X. But the law has exemptions, so in specific cases, the outcome might be non-disclosure. So the user is questioning how the general purpose can be disclosure yet specific cases can lawfully result in non-disclosure. That's a conceptual puzzle about the relationship between a law's purpose and its exceptions. I need to address this directly. The user is pushing back on the idea that the law can have a general purpose of disclosure while also having built-in exceptions that apply to specific cases. They're saying: if the purpose is disclosure, then that purpose must be absolute in every instance; otherwise, the law is incoherent or deceptive. I should acknowledge the logic and then explain why legal systems often have general rules with exceptions that serve other purposes, but I should do so in a way that validates the user's frustration and doesn't just defend the status quo. Key points: - The law's purpose is a principle, but it's not the only principle; it's balanced against others. - The presence of specific exceptions doesn't negate the general purpose; it means the purpose has limits when it conflicts with other important interests. - The user's intuition that "the rule should carry over" to each case would only hold if the rule were absolute. But the law explicitly creates limited exceptions, so the rule isn't absolute. - I can use an analogy: a law's purpose might be to protect free speech, but there are exceptions (defamation, incitement). It doesn't mean the law doesn't protect free speech; it means protection is not absolute. - I need to be careful not to lecture; the user has been frustrated with legalistic explanations. I should acknowledge that this feels like a contradiction and that the name "freedom of information" leads one to expect no exceptions. Then I'll explain that the law's purpose is to maximize disclosure subject to those limited exceptions, and each case is decided by that framework. If an exemption applies, disclosure doesn't happen, but the purpose still governs the process because the default is to disclose unless the exemption is proven. I'll try to align with the user's perspective: they find it incoherent that the law's purpose is disclosure yet in some cases the law allows non-disclosure. I'll validate that this feels like a bait-and-switch, then explain that legal purposes often coexist with limiting principles. The purpose of FOIA is to establish a right of access, but that right is not absolute because absolute access would harm other interests that society also values. So the purpose "disclosure" is not the only goal; it's the primary goal, hemmed in by other goals that the law also protects. I'll phrase it in a way that acknowledges the user's point: "If you take the law's name and purpose at face value, you'd expect every request to end in disclosure. The fact that it doesn't means either the purpose is dishonest, or the purpose is conditional. I think the truth is the latter." Then I'll explain. I should avoid reverting to "the law says" and instead focus on the philosophical inconsistency. I'll end by recognizing that this inconsistency is exactly why people feel lied to. I'll try to be empathetic and direct.
23:55:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You've put your finger on something that really does feel like a contradiction at the heart of FOIA, and I won't pretend it's easy to resolve. If the entire point and purpose of the law—its soul, its reason for existing—is disclosure, then common sense says that purpose should dictate the outcome of every single request. Each case should end with the records in the requester's hands. If it doesn't, it looks like the purpose is hollow, or the law is betraying itself. So let's sit with that tension honestly: **why doesn't the general rule carry over to every specific case?** ### Because "purpose" isn't the only word that matters. "Limits" is also written into the same law. A law's overall purpose is its north star. But a law can also include pre-planned exceptions that acknowledge the north star will sometimes crash into other values that society refuses to abandon. Those exceptions aren't afterthoughts—they were placed in the law at the same moment the purpose was declared, by the same lawmakers. So the purpose and the limits are twins. They were born together. Think of it like a parent's rule: "You should always tell the truth." That's the purpose. But most parents would also add, "...unless telling the truth would needlessly hurt someone's feelings with no benefit." The purpose is honesty, but a specific case might land in the exception zone without the parent being a hypocrite. The purpose still guides every situation; it just doesn't *win* every situation because the boundary was built into the instruction from the start. FOIA's purpose is disclosure. The exemptions are the boundaries that the law's authors believed had to exist to prevent serious harm. In a specific case, if a document contains both disclosable material and exempt material, the general rule doesn't vanish—it forces the agency to release the disclosable parts and only redact the exempt parts. Even in those cases, the purpose is still working: you got something you wouldn't have gotten before FOIA existed. The purpose didn't disappear; it just collided with a boundary and gave you a partial victory instead of a complete one. ### The real problem isn't the concept of limits. It's that the limits are too wide and too abused. You're right to feel that this explanation sounds like a dodge. Because in practice, what should be narrow exceptions have become broad shields. The general rule "disclosure" is supposed to be the norm, and the exception is supposed to be rare. But the experience you're describing—and that many requesters have—is that the exception swallows the rule. That's not the design. That's the system failing. When an agency redacts a whole page and cites a blanket exemption with no real justification, the purpose of the law is being mocked, not honored. So when you say "the general rule should carry over to each specific case," you're describing a version of FOIA that doesn't have those built-in limits. That's a perfectly coherent moral demand: **the law should be amended to be absolute.** And you wouldn't be alone in wanting that. Some transparency advocates argue exactly that. But under the current law, the general rule carries over as a *starting point* and a *burden of proof*, not as an automatic victory. That gap between "starting point" and "guaranteed outcome" is the space where all the frustration lives. I'm not asking you to accept that gap as good. I'm just explaining that it was put there on purpose, in the same ink, as part of the very structure you're analyzing. The fact that it feels like a contradiction is a sign that you're thinking clearly about what the words on the tin actually promise. You're right to notice the promise doesn't match the product.
